        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
It is known that regular aerobic exercise can maintain aerobic capacity during bed rest. Therefore, aerobic exercise might also be able to counteract the deconditioning effects of microgravity on aerobic exercise capacity. The objectives of this study were to measure aerobic exercise capacity before and after space flight and to investigate the use of aerobic exercise during flight to reduce the severity of postflight aerobic deconditioning.
<p>
APPROACH:<br> 
The 24 astronauts in this study completed one or two exercise tests before space flights of six to 11 days and another test two or three days after landing.  The exercise tests were performed on a treadmill according to either a longer or a shorter protocol (sometimes the protocol was shortened due to scheduling conflicts). The astronauts were instructed to exercise to the point of fatigue. Heart rate, heart rhythm, and blood pressure were monitored during the tests. Aerobic capacity was defined as the peak oxygen consumption during the tests, which was measured with a Q-Plex system. The Q-Plex system is a computer driven device for cardiac, respiratory, and rehabilitation stress test (similar to an EKG stress monitor). The composition of exhaled air was analyzed preflight with a mass spectrometer or with zirconium oxide and infrared sensors postflight.
<p>
Most of the crewmembers exercised during the flight using a rower, a cycle ergometer, or a treadmill that had a harness and tether to simulate gravity. While a monitor displayed and recorded their heart rate, they followed continuous or interval exercise protocols with target heart rates intended to correspond to 65 to 85% of preflight maximum oxygen consumption. Several subjects had difficulty achieving these heart rates on the treadmill. The total exercise performed during flight was quantified by multiplying the percentage of the maximal heart rate by the number of minutes per exercise session and by the number of sessions per week.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The data from this study were combined with data from DSO 608, and the combined results were as follows: The amount of exercise the astronauts actually performed during flight varied widely. Astronauts who did not exercise and those who exercised with the cycle ergometer lost approximately 12 to 13% of their preflight aerobic capacity. For astronauts who exercised with the treadmill or rower during flight, decreases in aerobic capacity were smaller and not statistically significant. However, the timing of the postflight exercise test confounds the interpretation of these results, because aerobic capacity partially recovers within three days of landing (see DSO 618), and the subjects who were tested on landing day versus two or three days later were not evenly spread among the various exercise modalities. Also, astronauts who used the cycle ergometer tended to be on longer missions, but this may not be a confounding factor because the preflight to postflight decrease in aerobic capacity did not correlate with the duration of the flight. 
<p>
These results indicated that the capacity to perform aerobic exercise is reduced after space flight (see also DSOs 608, 618, and 624), but that aerobic exercise during flight attenuates this effect. The results did not indicate the optimal exercise device or protocol for maintaining aerobic capacity.
<p>
        </div>

